# State Schemas

This Livebook declares a complete Agent data schema with defaults and a
cross-field rule.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, path: Path.expand("..", __DIR__)}
])
```

## Declare A State Contract

```elixir
defmodule StateSchemas.CounterState do
  def schema do
    Zoi.object(%{
      count: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0),
      minimum: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0),
      maximum: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(10)
    })
    |> Zoi.refine({__MODULE__, :within_bounds, []})
  end

  def within_bounds(%{count: count, minimum: minimum, maximum: maximum}, _opts) do
    if minimum <= count and count <= maximum do
      :ok
    else
      {:error, "count must be within the ordered bounds"}
    end
  end
end
```

Keep the data schema static. The refinement uses a named module function, so the
declaration remains explicit. State schemas contain defaults and validation
rules. They reject transforms, coercion, codecs, string-to-boolean conversion,
and lazy schemas. Convert external input before you create state, or use an
Action input schema for that conversion. A successful refinement must leave
its value unchanged. The same rules apply to Plugin-owned state.

## Declare The Agent

```elixir
defmodule StateSchemas.Counter do
  use Jido.Agent, name: "state_schema_counter"

  agent do
    schema StateSchemas.CounterState.schema()
  end
end
```

## Apply Defaults

```elixir
agent = StateSchemas.Counter.new!(id: "bounded-counter")
agent.state
```

The result is `%{count: 0, minimum: 0, maximum: 10}`.

Initialization parses supplied values and defaults in one traversal. Nested
defaults pass through their type checks and refinements. Each refinement runs
once per value in that traversal. A default of `nil` needs a nullable schema.
Optional missing fields stay absent; explicit `nil` selects their default.
Supplied nested maps merge with declared initial map defaults.

Instance, candidate, and restored-state checks do not apply defaults or change
values. A missing required field, including a defaulted field, is an error.
Explicit `nil` must satisfy the stored-value schema. Defaults apply only when
an instance is created.

## Reject An Invalid Instance

```elixir
{:error, error} =
  StateSchemas.Counter.new(
    id: "invalid-counter",
    state: %{count: 11, minimum: 0, maximum: 10}
  )

error.__struct__
```

Instantiation rejects the complete state. The same data schema runs for direct
commands and live candidates. Checkpoint conversion can change the saved
representation. Restore validates the reconstructed live state after conversion.

Live state can contain local values when its schema permits them. Durable
checkpoints must contain portable values after conversion. Keep resource
lifecycle and ownership in Plugin runtimes or application supervision.

Continue with [Plugin-Owned State](plugin-state.md) and
[Portable State And Checkpoints](portable-state-and-checkpoints.md).

## Test object intersections

Run the generated intersection cases with:

```sh
mix test.property
mix test.fuzz
```

The cases cover branch order, nested defaults, overlapping fields, conflicts,
missing stored fields, nullable values, and refinement call counts. CI,
`mix quality`, and `mix test.all` include the property cases. The longer fuzz
run is separate.
